  "body": "## What this changes\n\nNine values deciding how `mcc-desktop` behaves were literals in the source, and a Chromium installed anywhere unusual was **undiscoverable** because a hardcoded candidate list was the only way a browser could be found.\n\nAll nine become settings, riding the machinery that already exists for the fallback and retention limits rather than a parallel one — a `LimitRange` in the shared table, a `Settings` field, a manifest entry, an `.env.example` block.\n\n| Setting | Default | Range | Why the bound |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `DESKTOP_HEALTH_CHECK_INTERVAL` | 0.25 | 0.05–5 | floor stops a busy loop |\n| `DESKTOP_SERVER_START_TIMEOUT` | 15 | 1–300 | |\n| `DESKTOP_ADMIN_REQUEST_TIMEOUT` | 5 | 0.5–60 | loopback, so both ends stay short |\n| `DESKTOP_ACTIVATION_POLL_SECONDS` | 1 | 0.1–3600 | floor stops a busy loop |\n| `DESKTOP_HEALTH_POLL_SECONDS` | 5 | 0.5–3600 | floor stops a busy loop |\n| `DESKTOP_HEALTH_FAILURE_THRESHOLD` | 3 | 1–1000 | this is what debounces a self-update restart |\n| `DESKTOP_WINDOW_WIDTH` | 1400 | 640–7680 | 640 keeps the dashboard usable |\n| `DESKTOP_WINDOW_HEIGHT` | 900 | 480–4320 | |\n| `DESKTOP_BROWSER_PATH` | `\"\"` | — | explicit Chromium path |\n\nThe shared table is the point: the form **rejects** out-of-range input and quotes the range, `Settings` **clamps** at load with a warning, and both read the same entry — so the form cannot accept a value the runtime will quietly change.\n\n`\"1400,900\"` split into width and height because a single string cannot be range-validated. `DESKTOP_BROWSER_PATH` removes an assumption rather than a constant: set it and it wins; point it at nothing and it logs and falls through to the search.\n\n**Deliberately left hardcoded**, with the reason: `desktop.lock` / `desktop.activate` (internal file names), `LINUX_WM_CLASS` (window-manager identity), the profile directory name, the server module import path. \"As little hardcoded as possible\" needs a boundary or it ends with a configurable application name.\n\nEvery field description states that `mcc-desktop` reads these **once at launch**, so a change applies to the next tray start. A setting that appears to do nothing is worse than one that is absent.\n\n## A defect caught in review\n\nThe fields were registered as a `desktop` manifest section that **no dashboard view claimed** — so all nine would have been served by the API and **rendered nowhere**. Schema, persistence and serialization all working while the page stayed blank. This project shipped that exact gap once before, as a settings page with no page.\n\nFixed, and then made impossible to repeat: a new contract test asserts every manifest section is claimed by some view, and every key a view claims exists. It parses the shipped `admin.js` rather than a Python mirror — **a mirror would agree with itself while the page stayed blank**. It immediately found a second, pre-existing orphan (`smoke`), recorded in a documented allow-list rather than hidden by weakening the assertion.\n\nMutation-tested: unclaiming `desktop` fails it by name.\n\n## Verification\n\n**Full suite: 6 failed, 4350 passed, 26 skipped** — the six documented PowerShell installer failures, identical to unmodified `fork/main` in the same shell (`6 failed, 4294 passed`). Zero new failures. ruff format, ruff check, ty: clean.\n\n**Through the real admin API on a live server:**\n\n```\nwidth 100  (floor 640) -> valid:false, \"DESKTOP_WINDOW_WIDTH: accepts 640 to 7680.\"\nwidth 1600             -> applied, persisted to .env\nwidth blank            -> written as KEY=, server still answers 200,\n                          and a fresh Settings() load reads 1400\n```\n\nThat blank case is not academic — a cleared field in the admin UI writes `KEY=`, and refusing to parse it once stopped this server booting. All nine fields confirmed served under section `desktop`, each carrying its range.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- The desktop process reads these at launch, so a change needs a tray restart. Stated in every description, but it is a real ergonomic wrinkle.\n- The window size feeds `--window-size` for app-mode; a value the OS rejects is the OS's business, not ours.\n- `smoke` remains unrendered by design and is now explicitly recorded rather than silently orphaned.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
