    "message": "feat(desktop): make the desktop process configurable instead of hardcoded\n\nNine values that decide how mcc-desktop behaves were literals in the source:\nthe health poll interval and failure threshold, the activation poll, the\nserver start timeout, the admin request timeout, the startup health check\ninterval, and the window size as the string \"1400,900\". A Chromium installed\nanywhere unusual was undiscoverable, because a hardcoded candidate list was\nthe only way a browser could be found.\n\nAll nine are now settings, and they ride the machinery that already exists for\nthe fallback and retention limits rather than a parallel one: a LimitRange in\nthe shared table, a Settings field, a manifest entry, and an .env.example\nblock. That shared table is the point -- the admin form rejects an\nout-of-range value and quotes the range, Settings clamps at load with a\nwarning, and both read the same entry, so the form cannot accept a value the\nruntime will quietly change.\n\n\"1400,900\" became DESKTOP_WINDOW_WIDTH and DESKTOP_WINDOW_HEIGHT because a\nsingle string cannot be range-validated.\n\nDESKTOP_BROWSER_PATH is the one that removes an assumption rather than a\nconstant. When set it wins over the candidate search; when it points at\nsomething that does not exist it logs and falls through to the search, because\na guardrail must degrade rather than become an outage.\n\nDeliberately left hardcoded, with the reason: desktop.lock and\ndesktop.activate (internal file names), LINUX_WM_CLASS (window-manager\nidentity, like the app name), the profile directory name, and the server\nmodule import path. \"As little hardcoded as possible\" needs a boundary or it\nends with a configurable application name.\n\nEvery field description states that mcc-desktop is a separate process that\nreads these once at launch, so a change applies to the next tray start rather\nthan to a window already open. A setting that appears to do nothing is worse\nthan one that is absent.\n\nA defect was caught in review and fixed. The new fields were registered as a\n\"desktop\" manifest section that NO dashboard view claimed, so all nine would\nhave been served by the API and rendered nowhere -- schema, persistence and\nserialization all working while the page stayed blank. That exact gap shipped\nonce before as a settings page with no page. The section is now claimed by the\nLimits view, and a new contract test makes the class of bug impossible to\nrepeat: every manifest section must be claimed by some view, and every key a\nview claims must exist. It reads the shipped admin.js rather than a Python\nmirror, because a mirror would agree with itself while the page stayed blank.\nIt immediately found a second, pre-existing orphan (the smoke section), which\nis recorded in a documented allow-list rather than hidden by weakening the\nassertion.\n\nVerification:\n\n- Full suite: 6 failed, 4350 passed, 26 skipped -- the six documented\n  PowerShell installer failures, identical to unmodified fork/main measured in\n  the same shell (6 failed, 4294 passed). Zero new failures.\n- ruff format, ruff check, ty: clean.\n- Live server on a throwaway home, through the real admin API:\n      width 100 (floor 640) -> valid:false, \"accepts 640 to 7680.\"\n      width 1600            -> applied, persisted to .env\n      width blank           -> written as KEY=, server still answers 200,\n                               and a fresh Settings() load reads 1400\n  That blank case is not academic: a cleared field in the admin UI writes\n  KEY=, and refusing to parse it once stopped the server booting.\n- All nine fields confirmed served in /admin/api/config under section\n  \"desktop\", each carrying its range.\n- The section guard was mutation-tested: unclaiming \"desktop\" fails it by name.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
